#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229993} :activity/id #uuid "02989bdb-eb33-5669-a5fd-ba6ae2e8e96d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050537 :topic/id #uuid "51e12769-3848-5c84-bd86-1c35f7ad69b3" :topic/math-academy-id 6707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230210 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $D$ is an $n$th-order differential operator, and the functions $y, y_1,$ and $y_2$ are $n$-times differentiable in some domain of interest. \n\nWe say that a differential operator $D$ is **linear** if the following conditions hold:\n\n- $D(a y(x)) = a D(y(x)),$ where $a$ is a constant.\n\n- $D(y_1(x) + y_2(x)) = D(y_1(x)) + D(y_2(x)).$\n\nWe can combine these two conditions into a single condition as follows:\n\n$$\nD(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aD(y_1(x)) + bD(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIt's worth taking a moment to compare these conditions to those we used when defining a *linear transformation*\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: \\mathbf v \\rightarrow \\mathbf w.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor $\\mathbf T$ to be linear, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(a\\mathbf{v})=a\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and any scalar $a.$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1+\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)+\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_2)$ for any vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nThe conditions are essentially the same in both cases! Only instead of a transformation $\\mathbf T,$ we have a differential operator $D.$ And instead of vectors as our inputs and outputs, we have functions.\n\nNext, we'll apply this definition to a specific differential operator to see if it is linear." :tutorial/id #uuid "7af65e32-6bf5-5cbf-868a-c08401a21ff4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14617 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "84f3814e-98a9-5834-ac22-59909528ef84" :step/math-academy-id 591452 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230211 :tutorial/content "A differential operator $D$ is **linear** if the following linearity condition is satisfied.\n\n$$\nD(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aD(y_1(x)) + bD(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- First, let's consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD_1(y) = x\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} +x^2y\n$$\n\nThis operator is indeed linear. To see this, we need to check that the linearity condition above holds. So we start by applying our operator to the function $y = a y_1(x) + b y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince derivatives distribute over sums, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1})+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1})+bx\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{2})+a{x}^{2}{y}_{1}+b{x}^{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a{{(x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}{y}_{1})}_{⏟}}_{{D}_{1}({y}_{1})}+b{{(x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}{y}_{2})}_{⏟}}_{{D}_{1}({y}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, since $D_1(ay_1 + by_2) = aD_1(y_1) + bD_1(y_2),$ the operator $D_1$ is linear.\n\n- Now, let's consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD_2(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nThis operator is *not* linear. To see this, we need to check that the linearity condition above does not hold. So we start by applying our operator to the function $y = a y_1(x) + b y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to $aD_2(y_1) + bD_2(y_2),$ we see that they are *not* the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2}) & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\ne {D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the operator $D_2$ is *not* linear." :tutorial/id #uuid "423cbd55-072d-53de-9269-6dc635abd107" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14620 :tutorial/title "The Linearity Condition"} :step/id #uuid "217461ee-fef0-5c90-a0c9-17bb7b4eec7e" :step/math-academy-id 591453 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229995}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229137 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228952 :question/id #uuid "d01adef6-d3fb-5cd6-b778-5d44fb6702dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6572"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048459}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404044}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"} :step/id #uuid "60f2a01e-93e7-50c5-9aeb-84890396ee17" :step/math-academy-id 591454 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229996}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230212 :tutorial/content "An **$n$th-order linear differential operator**, denoted $L$, has the general form:\n\n$$\nL(y) = f_{n}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n}y}{\\textrm d x^{n}} + f_{n-1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n-1}y}{\\textrm d x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y.\n$$\n\nThe functions $f_i(x)$ are called the **coefficient functions**. They must depend only on the variable $x$ and be continuous on the domain of interest. The **order** of the operator is the order of the highest derivative whose coefficient is not zero.\n\nIf all the coefficient functions $f_i(x)$ are constants, we call the operator a **linear operator with constant coefficients**.\n\nLet's look at two examples:\n\n- The operator $L(y) = x^2 y'' - (\\sin x) y' + 2y$ is a 2nd-order *linear differential operator*. Its coefficient functions are $f_2(x) = x^2$, $f_1(x) = -\\sin x$, and $f_0(x) = 2$.\n\n- The operator $L(y) = 4y''' + 7y'' - y$ is a 3rd-order *linear operator with constant coefficients*. Its coefficient functions are $f_3(x) = 4$, $f_2(x) = 7$, $f_1(x) = 0$, and $f_0(x) = -1$." :tutorial/id #uuid "ea3a57ac-0a91-5f3d-85bc-303fef4eb21f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14623 :tutorial/title "The General Form of a Linear Differential Operator"} :step/id #uuid "93ca49af-1d89-5a5d-8223-c645852994cc" :step/math-academy-id 591457 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229997}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228933 :question/id #uuid "3c82270a-33f2-53ee-8876-9d8c7cf8291b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21786"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311917}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"} :step/id #uuid "8c4cc306-2777-59aa-b11f-63020aec529d" :step/math-academy-id 591458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229139 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228951 :question/id #uuid "04f55d6c-825e-587c-8ef4-a5527d1f374e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6569"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348246}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"} :step/id #uuid "11bd78b5-5bc9-5c73-a208-5e08533f0ffd" :step/math-academy-id 591461}] :activity/title "Linear Differential Operators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229264}]}
